1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:12,300
Some say he was the greatest inventor of all time, even beating out Edison to electrify

2
00:00:12,301 --> 00:00:13,300
the planet.

3
00:00:13,301 --> 00:00:16,980
His name was known around the world after this event.

4
00:00:16,981 --> 00:00:22,540
But was he the mad genius who revolutionized the modern world, or was he just mad?

5
00:00:22,541 --> 00:00:26,240
At one point he actually referred to one bird as his wife.

6
00:00:26,241 --> 00:00:29,660
Now, the shocking truth behind Nikola Tesla.

7
00:00:29,661 --> 00:00:32,720
You'll never flip on a light switch the same way again

8
00:00:32,721 --> 00:00:33,840
on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:51,600
He was a true American hero.

10
00:00:51,601 --> 00:00:54,940
But after a while, people forgot about what he did.

11
00:00:54,941 --> 00:00:57,320
I believe that he was the most important inventor

12
00:00:57,321 --> 00:00:58,840
in human history.

13
00:00:58,841 --> 00:01:01,080
Some of his neighbors did think he was absolutely crazy

14
00:01:01,081 --> 00:01:03,180
and thought he was a danger to their community.

15
00:01:03,181 --> 00:01:04,920
I think that he had a different version

16
00:01:04,921 --> 00:01:06,540
of what the future would look like.

17
00:01:06,541 --> 00:01:08,880
He's responsible for the 20th century.

18
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:19,120
For the power to change night into day,

19
00:01:20,140 --> 00:01:21,480
thank Nikola Tesla.

20
00:01:21,481 --> 00:01:29,400
It's clear there are so many aspects of modern life, everyday life that is directly influenced

21
00:01:29,401 --> 00:01:33,180
or created by Nikola Tesla.

22
00:01:33,181 --> 00:01:38,900
His alternating current or AC power system paved the way for all of our modern electric

23
00:01:38,901 --> 00:01:39,900
conveniences.

24
00:01:39,901 --> 00:01:48,160
He's basically responsible for our entire system of electricity, our entire modern world.

25
00:01:48,161 --> 00:01:54,160
But tragically, in later years, his brilliance, responsible for more than 200 patents, was

26
00:01:54,161 --> 00:01:57,160
overshadowed by what some considered madness.

27
00:01:57,161 --> 00:02:03,760
He was often seen as sort of the mad scientist who was out of control and would destroy the

28
00:02:03,761 --> 00:02:11,020
world when he really wanted to improve the lot of humanity.

29
00:02:11,021 --> 00:02:16,620
Because of Tesla, a turn of the switch brings power to our fingertips.

30
00:02:16,621 --> 00:02:21,060
Yet the same intellect fanatically rescued injured pigeons,

31
00:02:21,061 --> 00:02:23,700
was repulsed by women's jewelry,

32
00:02:23,701 --> 00:02:27,160
and claimed to receive communications from outer space.

33
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:33,740
Our virtues and our failings are inseparable,

34
00:02:33,741 --> 00:02:36,180
like force and matter.

35
00:02:36,181 --> 00:02:40,180
When they separate, man is no more.

36
00:02:40,181 --> 00:02:42,120
Nikola Tesla.

37
00:02:42,121 --> 00:02:46,560
At the beginning of the 20th century,

38
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:49,460
Tesla's amazing accomplishments were celebrated.

39
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,780
But he died in 1943 at the New Yorker Hotel

40
00:02:54,781 --> 00:02:56,940
in this small room,

41
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:00,660
virtually destitute and discredited

42
00:03:00,661 --> 00:03:03,660
as he watched the world he helped create

43
00:03:03,661 --> 00:03:05,160
simply forget him.

44
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:12,100
Nevertheless, the FBI amassed a secret file on Tesla.

45
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:17,120
And when he died, the United States government took charge of his papers.

46
00:03:17,121 --> 00:03:22,120
If you listen to everything he talked about, it was so powerful.

47
00:03:22,121 --> 00:03:24,120
It was so frightening.

48
00:03:24,121 --> 00:03:31,120
It was so terrifying that they just pounced upon all this possession and whisked them away.

49
00:03:31,121 --> 00:03:37,120
What were the powerful and terrifying ideas that may have threatened national security?

50
00:03:37,121 --> 00:03:41,120
Were they a madman's rantings or works of genius?

51
00:03:41,121 --> 00:03:45,493
of genius. There is still a place for Nikola Tesla's contributions.

52
00:03:45,494 --> 00:03:47,060
Many people believe that

53
00:03:47,061 --> 00:03:48,812
he was actually onto something.

54
00:03:48,813 --> 00:03:52,260
Maybe it's time to look back and reinvestigate what Tesla was

55
00:03:52,261 --> 00:03:53,249
really up to.

56
00:03:53,250 --> 00:03:59,560
We know one thing Tesla was up to, wireless energy that could be transmitted around

57
00:03:59,561 --> 00:04:00,261
the world.

58
00:04:00,262 --> 00:04:06,140
The wireless transmission of power would appear over and over again, even in Tesla's

59
00:04:06,141 --> 00:04:09,320
most exotic visions.

60
00:04:09,321 --> 00:04:14,960
The key to understanding this remarkable idea was this peculiar yet powerful invention,

61
00:04:14,961 --> 00:04:21,920
first patented in 1891 by Tesla at the age of 35, called the Tesla coil.

62
00:04:21,921 --> 00:04:24,560
Nothing like it had ever been done before.

63
00:04:24,561 --> 00:04:27,840
Think of the Tesla coil as a giant electrical pump.

64
00:04:27,841 --> 00:04:30,620
This is an example of a modern-day Tesla coil.

65
00:04:30,621 --> 00:04:34,640
We're going to take power from a wall outlet, feed it to the base of the unit, where we're

66
00:04:34,641 --> 00:04:39,340
We're going to step up the voltage tremendously until it comes out the top.

67
00:04:39,341 --> 00:04:44,360
So I'm going to step back and we're going to take a look.

68
00:04:44,361 --> 00:04:51,400
This Tesla coil steps up standard power from a wall outlet at 120 volts to more than 500,000

69
00:04:51,401 --> 00:04:55,160
volts.

70
00:04:55,161 --> 00:05:01,080
Imagine electricity flowing in a wire the same way as water pumped through a hose.

71
00:05:01,081 --> 00:05:03,780
The current can be likened to the flow of water.

72
00:05:03,781 --> 00:05:06,820
The voltage can be likened to water pressure.

73
00:05:06,821 --> 00:05:10,940
If a nozzle was attached to the hose, the force of the water pressure is dramatically

74
00:05:10,941 --> 00:05:15,580
increased while the flow of water is decreased.

75
00:05:15,581 --> 00:05:18,180
A Tesla coil acts in the same way.

76
00:05:18,181 --> 00:05:23,720
By passing the current from a small primary coil to a secondary, much larger coil, it

77
00:05:23,721 --> 00:05:29,280
steps up the voltage tremendously and reduces the current.

78
00:05:29,281 --> 00:05:33,240
This makes the Tesla coil a transformer.

79
00:05:33,241 --> 00:05:38,140
Much like firefighters would connect a big hose to a fire hydrant and then use the nozzle

80
00:05:38,141 --> 00:05:44,600
to spray that water very far away, Tesla coil does the same thing.

81
00:05:44,601 --> 00:05:48,920
Using an enormous Tesla coil, the inventor wanted to pump the Earth an atmosphere full

82
00:05:48,921 --> 00:05:51,160
of electric energy.

83
00:05:51,161 --> 00:05:55,960
He believed he could use the Earth as a natural conductor of electricity and send power around

84
00:05:55,961 --> 00:05:57,860
the world.

85
00:05:57,861 --> 00:06:03,940
In essence, the Earth would become a giant electrical outlet.

86
00:06:03,941 --> 00:06:10,040
To prove his wildly original theory, Tesla left his home in New York City and began experimenting

87
00:06:10,041 --> 00:06:17,860
with wireless power in 1899, at the age of 43, on the windswept prairies of Colorado.

88
00:06:17,861 --> 00:06:24,560
He believed that there was some specific frequency at which the Earth could be struck as with

89
00:06:24,561 --> 00:06:26,180
a hammer.

90
00:06:26,181 --> 00:06:30,520
Tesla believed that the Earth could be rung electrically,

91
00:06:30,521 --> 00:06:35,180
just as a bell can be rung with a clapper.

92
00:06:35,181 --> 00:06:37,060
He constructed a laboratory and tower

93
00:06:37,061 --> 00:06:41,160
that soared 80 feet into the air.

94
00:06:41,161 --> 00:06:45,360
Inside the barn-like structure was an enormous Tesla coil.

95
00:06:45,361 --> 00:06:48,860
For Tesla to be creating these electrical arcs

96
00:06:48,861 --> 00:06:52,380
in his laboratory was very unsettling to the people

97
00:06:52,381 --> 00:06:57,220
Colorado because they felt that he was conjuring the work of God.

98
00:07:00,860 --> 00:07:04,600
By tapping into the local electrical power from Colorado Springs,

99
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:08,320
Tesla's coil produced more than 12 million volts.

100
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:14,820
He often demonstrated his wireless concept by illuminating a phosphorescent bulb while

101
00:07:14,821 --> 00:07:15,880
holding it in his hand.

102
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:22,280
When the machine is on, the entire lab is filled with high-voltage electricity.

103
00:07:22,380 --> 00:07:25,180
I'm going to grab this fluorescent light and show you what I mean.

104
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:36,460
The Tesla coil outputs electric energy without harming humans,

105
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:40,480
similar to the way transmission towers bombard us with radio waves.

106
00:07:42,520 --> 00:07:48,120
What you're seeing is electricity fly through the air from the Tesla coil to the bulb lighting it.

107
00:07:48,320 --> 00:07:50,620
That's the essence of Tesla's original idea.

108
00:07:50,621 --> 00:07:55,800
He wanted to transmit power globally so that people need only receive it to use it.

109
00:07:57,460 --> 00:08:00,780
Tesla claimed his experiments in Colorado were a success,

110
00:08:01,420 --> 00:08:06,980
and he achieved the transmission of power, illuminating light bulbs up to one mile away.

111
00:08:10,820 --> 00:08:14,760
In addition to pronouncements of his Colorado electrical discoveries,

112
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:17,680
Tesla also made an even more startling claim,

113
00:08:17,681 --> 00:08:20,520
that he received messages from outer space.

114
00:08:20,980 --> 00:08:22,980
He got a lot of flack when he came back to New York

115
00:08:22,981 --> 00:08:26,040
for saying, I've spoken to Mars, talking to Martians.

116
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:29,500
But it's interesting because the ideas that he was using

117
00:08:29,501 --> 00:08:32,000
are exactly the ideas that we're using now

118
00:08:32,001 --> 00:08:34,000
to send messages out into outer space.

119
00:08:34,001 --> 00:08:36,160
We're sending radio waves out into outer space

120
00:08:36,161 --> 00:08:37,840
to try to see if anyone's out there.

121
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:40,540
So people back then really had some idea

122
00:08:40,541 --> 00:08:41,420
that he might be crazy,

123
00:08:41,620 --> 00:08:44,000
but he was actually probably really ahead of his time.

124
00:08:44,001 --> 00:08:48,280
However, Tesla believed he had crossed a threshold

125
00:08:48,281 --> 00:08:51,380
and proven he could send power wirelessly.

126
00:08:52,760 --> 00:08:56,600
He just had to find more money to build an even bigger transmitter.

127
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:06,020
Tesla returned to the East Coast on Long Island Sound.

128
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:10,760
In the town of Shoreham, a street sign marks the entrance

129
00:09:10,761 --> 00:09:13,480
to a now abandoned and forgotten location.

130
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:19,000
In 1901, based on his Colorado experiments,

131
00:09:19,001 --> 00:09:23,000
Tesla began to build his visionary wireless power network

132
00:09:23,001 --> 00:09:25,000
here on this site.

133
00:09:25,001 --> 00:09:28,000
He called it Wardenclyffe.

134
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,000
It was comprised of a laboratory and power station.

135
00:09:34,001 --> 00:09:38,000
Adjacent to it was a tremendous 187-foot tower.

136
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
Power from the plant was sent to a giant Tesla coil

137
00:09:43,001 --> 00:09:46,160
coil in the tower.

138
00:09:46,161 --> 00:09:50,120
Underneath the tower, the inventor sank huge shafts 120 feet

139
00:09:50,121 --> 00:09:53,820
into the soil to transmit the electrical current into the earth.

140
00:09:56,560 --> 00:09:58,880
This was to be the first of many transmitters

141
00:09:58,881 --> 00:10:00,920
in a system that would encircle the world

142
00:10:00,921 --> 00:10:03,220
with wireless energy.

143
00:10:03,221 --> 00:10:05,880
The vast amounts of electricity necessary

144
00:10:05,881 --> 00:10:08,920
would come from huge hydroelectric projects.

145
00:10:08,921 --> 00:10:10,280
A lot of people thought it was crazy

146
00:10:10,281 --> 00:10:12,400
because they couldn't understand what he was seeing

147
00:10:12,401 --> 00:10:14,440
and what he was envisioning for the future.

148
00:10:14,441 --> 00:10:17,200
He was able to conceive of things that other people weren't.

149
00:10:17,201 --> 00:10:18,280
He was a visionary.

150
00:10:19,740 --> 00:10:21,900
Nikola Tesla had an ingenious vision

151
00:10:21,901 --> 00:10:24,320
for a worldwide wireless power network.

152
00:10:24,321 --> 00:10:25,940
But to realize that dream,

153
00:10:25,941 --> 00:10:29,180
he needed to convince the most powerful banker in America,

154
00:10:29,181 --> 00:10:34,120
JP Morgan, to invest $150,000 in the project.

155
00:10:34,121 --> 00:10:35,580
Tesla promised the financier

156
00:10:35,581 --> 00:10:37,220
that the tower would make millions

157
00:10:38,520 --> 00:10:41,760
by also broadcasting messages, news, music,

158
00:10:41,761 --> 00:10:43,920
and even pictures to any part of the world.

159
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:51,040
This is the original foundation for Tesla's tower.

160
00:10:51,041 --> 00:10:54,760
If you look, you can see that it's an eight-sided figure.

161
00:10:54,761 --> 00:10:57,840
It had a huge hole down in the ground,

162
00:10:57,841 --> 00:11:01,500
right in front of where I'm standing, 120 feet down,

163
00:11:01,501 --> 00:11:04,240
and a spiral staircase went around it.

164
00:11:04,241 --> 00:11:09,200
And then up into the sky, 187 feet, was the tower.

165
00:11:09,201 --> 00:11:13,900
The tower was attached by huge timbers on bars like this

166
00:11:13,901 --> 00:11:17,400
at each of the points along the side foundation walls.

167
00:11:17,401 --> 00:11:20,240
It loomed over everything in the area.

168
00:11:20,241 --> 00:11:23,520
It was overwhelming when you think of the size and scope

169
00:11:23,521 --> 00:11:25,880
of such a huge, huge construction.

170
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:30,960
This is the actual lab and power plant at Wardenclyffe,

171
00:11:30,961 --> 00:11:33,360
where Tesla worked for more than four years

172
00:11:33,361 --> 00:11:36,160
trying to develop his wireless concept.

173
00:11:36,161 --> 00:11:38,100
It was purchased by a photographic company

174
00:11:38,101 --> 00:11:39,660
more than 60 years ago.

175
00:11:40,740 --> 00:11:44,120
After remodeling, only part of the outside lab remains.

176
00:11:45,220 --> 00:11:47,400
Really getting to go inside, this is so exciting.

177
00:11:47,401 --> 00:11:49,020
It hasn't been open in a while.

178
00:11:49,980 --> 00:11:51,740
Although the building hasn't been used

179
00:11:51,741 --> 00:11:54,040
in more than a decade, the owners agree

180
00:11:54,041 --> 00:11:58,720
to open the doors to modern marvels for a rare look inside.

181
00:11:58,721 --> 00:12:00,000
Wow.

182
00:12:01,180 --> 00:12:02,860
Oh, this is incredible.

183
00:12:02,861 --> 00:12:12,820
This is amazing to be in here where Tesla worked, where he spent time with his assistants.

184
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:19,940
It's just amazing that over 100 years ago, Tesla was walking in these very walls.

185
00:12:20,380 --> 00:12:23,800
His equipment was here. His dreams were alive.

186
00:12:24,240 --> 00:12:27,720
He was looking forward to what he hoped would be a very glorious future.

187
00:12:27,721 --> 00:12:33,140
In part, it was timing that ultimately doomed Wardenclyffe.

188
00:12:33,640 --> 00:12:37,860
On December 12th of 1901, as Tesla was working at his wireless network,

189
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:40,880
Guglielmo Marconi beat him to the punch

190
00:12:40,881 --> 00:12:45,260
and successfully transmitted a radio signal across the Atlantic.

191
00:12:46,260 --> 00:12:48,920
While Marconi's achievements were indeed a first,

192
00:12:49,540 --> 00:12:53,600
in reality he used 17 of Tesla's patents to accomplish this feat.

193
00:12:53,601 --> 00:12:57,900
Tesla is not only forgotten as the father of radio,

194
00:12:58,840 --> 00:13:01,540
Marconi's transmission sealed the fate of Wardenclyffe.

195
00:13:03,580 --> 00:13:07,400
Morgan was no longer interested in supporting Tesla's work here.

196
00:13:08,040 --> 00:13:09,080
Marconi already did it.

197
00:13:09,140 --> 00:13:10,700
Why should he keep supporting Tesla?

198
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:13,960
And of course, Tesla's plan was greater than Morgan knew.

199
00:13:14,940 --> 00:13:18,220
Tesla could see reason to continue the funding and continue the work,

200
00:13:18,221 --> 00:13:20,760
but it wasn't Morgan's plan.

201
00:13:20,761 --> 00:13:27,940
So in 1905, while still under construction and after some amazing electrical displays,

202
00:13:27,941 --> 00:13:32,840
the Wardenclyffe project was abandoned and later destroyed.

203
00:13:32,841 --> 00:13:38,120
The world was not prepared for it.

204
00:13:38,121 --> 00:13:40,400
It was too far ahead of its time.

205
00:13:40,401 --> 00:13:45,420
But the same laws will prevail in the end and make it a triumphal success.

206
00:13:45,421 --> 00:13:46,880
Nikola Tesla.

207
00:13:46,881 --> 00:13:50,280
But could Tesla have succeeded?

208
00:13:50,281 --> 00:13:54,280
The mainstream scientific community has grave doubts.

209
00:13:54,281 --> 00:14:01,280
Tesla's schemes for worldwide electrification was simply out of the question.

210
00:14:01,281 --> 00:14:08,280
It makes no sense from the standpoint of the energy involved and the inefficiency of it.

211
00:14:08,281 --> 00:14:11,280
Still, some aren't sure.

212
00:14:11,281 --> 00:14:14,280
I believe that the wireless transmission of power is possible.

213
00:14:14,281 --> 00:14:18,280
The chance that power could be transmitted around the globe

214
00:14:18,281 --> 00:14:22,280
without wires was too great a discovery to pass up.

215
00:14:22,281 --> 00:14:25,280
Jeff Parisi, a high-voltage technician,

216
00:14:25,281 --> 00:14:28,280
was part of a team that built one of the largest Tesla coils

217
00:14:28,281 --> 00:14:32,280
in modern times, the 13M.

218
00:14:34,280 --> 00:14:38,280
The reason the 13M was built was to give scientists the opportunity

219
00:14:38,281 --> 00:14:42,280
to actually investigate some of Tesla's wireless transmission theories

220
00:14:42,281 --> 00:14:45,780
theories to see if they're practical.

221
00:14:45,781 --> 00:14:49,080
The 13M generates close to 12 million volts of power.

222
00:14:52,080 --> 00:14:53,820
What we learned is that the machine we built

223
00:14:53,821 --> 00:14:55,620
is not large enough.

224
00:14:55,621 --> 00:14:58,520
It's actually a quarter scale version of what Tesla made

225
00:14:58,521 --> 00:14:59,940
in Colorado Springs.

226
00:14:59,941 --> 00:15:02,940
And it seems a machine that large is really necessary.

227
00:15:02,941 --> 00:15:05,440
We hope in the future that we'll have the funding

228
00:15:05,441 --> 00:15:08,580
to be able to build a full scale Tesla coil

229
00:15:08,581 --> 00:15:09,940
and prove his theories.

230
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:17,280
Today, we take our electrified world for granted.

231
00:15:17,281 --> 00:15:24,280
Energy that powers our needs is as close as the nearest outlet.

232
00:15:24,281 --> 00:15:31,280
According to legend, the man who made it all happen was born in Croatia in 1856,

233
00:15:31,281 --> 00:15:36,280
at the stroke of midnight, during an electrical storm.

234
00:15:36,281 --> 00:15:41,280
Tesla's immediately associated with thunder and lightning, with electricity.

235
00:15:41,281 --> 00:15:53,360
Tesla began his career as an electrical engineer with a telephone company in Budapest in 1881.

236
00:15:54,880 --> 00:15:57,580
One day, as he was walking through a park with a friend,

237
00:15:58,620 --> 00:16:04,620
Tesla suddenly envisioned a groundbreaking concept for a new electric motor and drew it out in the dirt.

238
00:16:06,580 --> 00:16:10,140
This simple illustration became the patent for the induction motor.

239
00:16:10,141 --> 00:16:14,220
which would go on to be the standard electric motor for the world.

240
00:16:16,440 --> 00:16:19,560
It's used for everything, from tools and appliances,

241
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:24,740
to hybrid cars, to industrial plants.

242
00:16:27,500 --> 00:16:31,020
The induction motor works by energizing coils of wire

243
00:16:31,021 --> 00:16:34,440
placed around a stationary frame, called the stator.

244
00:16:35,300 --> 00:16:38,460
This induces a current in the coils onto a rotor.

245
00:16:40,140 --> 00:16:44,780
The alternating current in the coils causes the poles of the magnetic fields around them

246
00:16:44,781 --> 00:16:46,780
to change between north and south.

247
00:16:48,280 --> 00:16:51,780
The resulting attraction and repulsion with the coils as they alternate

248
00:16:51,781 --> 00:16:53,480
causes the rotor to spin.

249
00:16:57,700 --> 00:17:01,900
And the exact same ideas that Nicola Tesla came up with a hundred years ago

250
00:17:01,901 --> 00:17:05,740
are still used today in induction motors such as this one.

251
00:17:05,741 --> 00:17:07,540
The motor is made of two parts.

252
00:17:07,541 --> 00:17:11,600
the rotor, and the coil.

253
00:17:12,360 --> 00:17:15,640
Within the coil, a north-south magnetic field rotates,

254
00:17:16,080 --> 00:17:19,220
and as a magnetic field rotates, the rotor follows it.

255
00:17:21,880 --> 00:17:23,780
Just like it did 100 years ago

256
00:17:23,781 --> 00:17:25,540
when Nikola Tesla made the first prototype.

257
00:17:29,660 --> 00:17:31,980
In 1884, at age 28,

258
00:17:32,380 --> 00:17:34,720
Tesla moved to America with little money

259
00:17:34,721 --> 00:17:37,420
and only a letter of recommendation from his boss,

260
00:17:37,540 --> 00:17:38,740
to Thomas Edison.

261
00:17:39,900 --> 00:17:41,540
The letters simply read,

262
00:17:42,420 --> 00:17:43,960
I know of two great men.

263
00:17:44,300 --> 00:17:45,140
You are one of them.

264
00:17:46,020 --> 00:17:47,660
This young man is the other.

265
00:17:49,620 --> 00:17:51,580
Edison hired the brilliant young engineer

266
00:17:51,581 --> 00:17:53,500
and eventually asked him to redesign

267
00:17:53,501 --> 00:17:55,260
his company's electric generators

268
00:17:55,261 --> 00:17:57,520
for a $50,000 bonus.

269
00:17:59,400 --> 00:18:01,280
After Tesla developed a number

270
00:18:01,281 --> 00:18:03,180
of enormously profitable new patents,

271
00:18:03,580 --> 00:18:05,280
he asked Edison for his bonus.

272
00:18:05,281 --> 00:18:09,520
Edison says, you've got to be kidding me.

273
00:18:10,080 --> 00:18:12,800
You've got a lot to learn about an American sense of humor,

274
00:18:12,801 --> 00:18:14,400
and he doesn't give him the $50,000.

275
00:18:15,280 --> 00:18:18,880
And Tesla turns around immediately and says, well, Mr. Edison, I resign.

276
00:18:20,960 --> 00:18:24,720
And so began a lifelong feud between the upstart young genius

277
00:18:24,721 --> 00:18:26,000
and the established inventor.

278
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:31,200
Tesla leaves Edison's, and he was digging ditches

279
00:18:31,760 --> 00:18:34,880
in New York City for a year, a very, very dark time.

280
00:18:34,881 --> 00:18:37,560
All of his dreams are in shambles at this point.

281
00:18:39,140 --> 00:18:42,720
While toiling to feed himself and raise capital for new inventions,

282
00:18:43,840 --> 00:18:46,980
Tesla was appalled at the web of power wires strung up in the city.

283
00:18:49,180 --> 00:18:53,620
The whole system was a nightmare of cables and wiring overhead.

284
00:18:54,060 --> 00:18:57,040
Matter of fact, in some areas it even blocked out the sun.

285
00:18:57,600 --> 00:19:02,880
The electrical system he found deficient was called direct current, or DC.

286
00:19:02,881 --> 00:19:08,960
Edison, Tesla's former employer, was a major investor in DC power.

287
00:19:10,960 --> 00:19:17,360
Tesla knew there was a better way and was determined to invent a new system that became the global standard,

288
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:20,760
alternating current, or AC.

289
00:19:23,100 --> 00:19:29,540
Here's a direct current cable that would carry about a million watts to light a typical New York City block.

290
00:19:30,100 --> 00:19:32,780
Using alternating current in Tesla's AC system,

291
00:19:33,240 --> 00:19:36,720
a small wire such as this could power the same amount of homes.

292
00:19:37,500 --> 00:19:39,560
Same amount of power, just look at the difference.

293
00:19:40,840 --> 00:19:43,620
The difference between AC and DC power

294
00:19:43,621 --> 00:19:47,200
is all about how electricity or electrons flow.

295
00:19:50,600 --> 00:19:52,300
For DC current to work,

296
00:19:52,760 --> 00:19:56,160
there must be a continuous and direct flow of electrons along a wire

297
00:19:56,161 --> 00:19:58,100
from negative to positive poles.

298
00:19:59,540 --> 00:20:09,280
When power is applied, electrons, as signified by the red band, move to the work, do the work, and then come all the way back to the generator.

299
00:20:10,120 --> 00:20:16,140
The problem with this process is that the electrons encounter resistance along these wires.

300
00:20:16,360 --> 00:20:19,520
It's difficult for electrons to travel these great distances.

301
00:20:20,080 --> 00:20:23,640
So most of the energy in the system is lost in the wire.

302
00:20:23,641 --> 00:20:33,640
Well, the way Thomas Edison envisioned his system, he would have to put a power plant just about every mile to keep the voltage steady along his DC power grid.

303
00:20:34,340 --> 00:20:36,613
In 1887, Tesla filed seven U.S.

304
00:20:36,614 --> 00:20:43,140
patents for another, more efficient and cheaper power system, called alternating current.

305
00:20:43,141 --> 00:20:54,180
What Tesla discovered in his AC system was it was not necessary to send the electrons all the way to the work and back again.

306
00:20:54,720 --> 00:21:00,040
In fact, he alternated the current, sending it back and forth.

307
00:21:00,860 --> 00:21:10,140
Tesla developed a system of AC generators that alternated electric current between negative and positive poles at 60 cycles per second.

308
00:21:10,141 --> 00:21:15,140
By sending AC through a transformer with virtually no power loss,

309
00:21:15,141 --> 00:21:18,140
he could step up the voltage and lower the current,

310
00:21:18,141 --> 00:21:23,140
so that AC could be efficiently transmitted hundreds of miles further than DC current.

311
00:21:25,140 --> 00:21:29,140
Millionaire entrepreneur George Westinghouse thought Tesla's inventions

312
00:21:29,141 --> 00:21:32,140
could be the key to long-distance power transmission.

313
00:21:33,140 --> 00:21:36,140
He purchased the patents for $60,000

314
00:21:36,141 --> 00:21:40,840
and a healthy share of stock in the Westinghouse Corporation.

315
00:21:41,640 --> 00:21:44,040
If the new AC system was successful,

316
00:21:44,800 --> 00:21:46,240
Tesla would be a rich man.

317
00:21:48,600 --> 00:21:52,860
Nikola Tesla became a citizen of the United States in 1891.

318
00:21:54,180 --> 00:21:56,740
The same year, an all-out war of the currents

319
00:21:56,741 --> 00:22:00,560
erupted between his AC and Edison's DC power.

320
00:22:02,340 --> 00:22:04,720
Edison launched a propaganda campaign

321
00:22:04,721 --> 00:22:06,920
that showed the dangers of A.C.

322
00:22:07,760 --> 00:22:11,520
The public, they had rather grotesque experiments

323
00:22:11,521 --> 00:22:14,280
where they would take animals and electrocute them

324
00:22:14,281 --> 00:22:15,640
with alternating current.

325
00:22:19,700 --> 00:22:21,780
Edison convinced New York State

326
00:22:21,781 --> 00:22:24,240
to use Tesla's and Westinghouse A.C. power

327
00:22:24,241 --> 00:22:27,460
for the very first electrocution in 1890.

328
00:22:28,760 --> 00:22:31,540
A reporter called it a gruesome spectacle,

329
00:22:32,180 --> 00:22:33,600
far worse than any hanging.

330
00:22:34,720 --> 00:22:38,720
Edison dubbed the technique Westinghousing.

331
00:22:38,721 --> 00:22:43,720
Whether it was electrocuting animals or electrocuting prisoners,

332
00:22:43,721 --> 00:22:47,720
the thrust was, this is something you don't want in your house.

333
00:22:47,721 --> 00:22:50,720
In 1893, despite the bad press,

334
00:22:50,721 --> 00:22:53,720
Tesla and the Westinghouse Corporation won the bid

335
00:22:53,721 --> 00:22:56,720
for illuminating the Chicago World's Fair,

336
00:22:56,721 --> 00:22:59,720
the first all-electric fair in history.

337
00:22:59,721 --> 00:23:03,720
Edison, who made an unsuccessful bid, was frustrated.

338
00:23:03,721 --> 00:23:06,100
was frustrated over losing this opportunity

339
00:23:06,101 --> 00:23:09,900
and refused to let Tesla use his patented light bulbs.

340
00:23:13,220 --> 00:23:16,700
So Tesla needed to come up with a new light bulb,

341
00:23:17,480 --> 00:23:20,800
make 250,000 of them in six months' time to light the fair.

342
00:23:21,320 --> 00:23:23,540
Edison's bulb had a screw base at the bottom.

343
00:23:24,060 --> 00:23:26,320
He patented the whole method of powering the bulb

344
00:23:26,321 --> 00:23:29,780
through this screw base and how to seal the vacuum off inside.

345
00:23:30,100 --> 00:23:32,780
Tesla's solution was to have a ground glass stopper

346
00:23:32,781 --> 00:23:33,740
in the bottom of the bulb.

347
00:23:34,100 --> 00:23:35,860
The wires passed through the stopper,

348
00:23:36,020 --> 00:23:38,640
and he was able to manufacture a bulb

349
00:23:38,641 --> 00:23:40,480
that didn't interfere with Edison's patents.

350
00:23:41,040 --> 00:23:43,160
Tesla was able to beat Edison in his own game

351
00:23:43,161 --> 00:23:45,760
by producing a lamp that was more easy to manufacture,

352
00:23:46,120 --> 00:23:49,160
and he was able to do so in a short amount of time.

353
00:23:50,360 --> 00:23:52,440
On May 1, 1893,

354
00:23:53,260 --> 00:23:55,280
President Grover Cleveland pushed a button,

355
00:23:55,780 --> 00:23:59,680
and more than 200,000 of Tesla's incandescent lamps

356
00:23:59,681 --> 00:24:01,440
illuminated the fairgrounds.

357
00:24:01,441 --> 00:24:07,480
It was a monumental success and ushered in the era of modern electric lighting.

358
00:24:08,800 --> 00:24:12,980
Tesla was hailed the mastermind and the genius behind making this possible,

359
00:24:13,380 --> 00:24:16,280
and his name was known around the world after this event.

360
00:24:17,320 --> 00:24:20,580
Tesla, filled with confidence after this victory over Edison.

361
00:24:24,580 --> 00:24:26,860
Millions have witnessed its stunning power.

362
00:24:26,861 --> 00:24:34,280
Niagara Falls can send more than 750,000 gallons of water over its crest every second,

363
00:24:34,980 --> 00:24:40,760
producing enough power to generate 2.4 million kilowatts of electricity.

364
00:24:41,840 --> 00:24:46,040
At that rate, the foals could power half of Las Vegas on a hot summer night.

365
00:24:48,640 --> 00:24:54,240
Ever since pioneers began building sawmills along the Niagara River in the 1700s,

366
00:24:54,241 --> 00:25:01,240
Many had envisioned grand new ways to harness the thundering power of the falls.

367
00:25:01,241 --> 00:25:08,240
In 1893, after witnessing Tesla's and Westinghouse's triumph at the Chicago World's Fair,

368
00:25:08,241 --> 00:25:15,240
the Niagara Falls Commission awarded them the contract to tap the falls for generating alternating current.

369
00:25:15,241 --> 00:25:21,080
The big problem with using Niagara Falls for electricity

370
00:25:21,081 --> 00:25:25,220
was that the major consumer of that electric power

371
00:25:25,221 --> 00:25:27,220
was Buffalo.

372
00:25:27,221 --> 00:25:29,860
Buffalo is about 20 miles or so.

373
00:25:29,861 --> 00:25:34,960
And using DC transmission was totally impractical

374
00:25:34,961 --> 00:25:37,400
because a repeater station would have

375
00:25:37,401 --> 00:25:40,560
to have been built every two miles.

376
00:25:40,561 --> 00:25:44,440
AC avoided all of that.

377
00:25:44,441 --> 00:25:50,260
At the Falls, Tesla designed and engineered a complex system of generators and transformers

378
00:25:50,261 --> 00:25:52,680
to produce his AC power.

379
00:25:52,681 --> 00:25:57,140
Tesla didn't write down everything.

380
00:25:57,141 --> 00:25:58,560
Most of his stuff was in his head.

381
00:25:58,561 --> 00:26:00,580
He had to do it verbally.

382
00:26:00,581 --> 00:26:02,480
Tesla was in his own little world, more or less.

383
00:26:02,481 --> 00:26:07,660
He would not explain things to other people, but his assistants would.

384
00:26:07,661 --> 00:26:13,880
In 1896, the first AC hydroelectric station for long-distance power transmission went

385
00:26:13,881 --> 00:26:22,840
online and Buffalo, New York became the premier city to be electrified by powered transmission.

386
00:26:22,841 --> 00:26:26,400
Tesla was not here when he actually flipped the switch, but he had so much confidence

387
00:26:26,401 --> 00:26:29,760
in his own work that he knew it would work.

388
00:26:29,761 --> 00:26:35,760
Today, new power plants have replaced Tesla's structures.

389
00:26:35,761 --> 00:26:40,640
The buildings of one of the most important historical sites in the modern world now stand

390
00:26:40,641 --> 00:26:43,280
forgotten like dinosaurs.

391
00:26:43,281 --> 00:26:45,520
For the man who envisioned it all,

392
00:26:45,521 --> 00:26:49,020
only an isolated statue marks his extraordinary achievement.

393
00:26:50,960 --> 00:26:55,560
I wonder how many people who come by here,

394
00:26:55,561 --> 00:27:00,360
past this statue, actually have any idea

395
00:27:00,361 --> 00:27:05,460
who Nikola Tesla was and what he did to shape their world.

396
00:27:07,880 --> 00:27:10,040
Today, our world is ablaze,

397
00:27:10,041 --> 00:27:13,000
thanks to Tesla's AC technology.

398
00:27:13,001 --> 00:27:15,820
He had won his final battle with Edison,

399
00:27:15,821 --> 00:27:18,900
but victory came at an enormous cost.

400
00:27:18,901 --> 00:27:20,820
Westinghouse was financially drained

401
00:27:20,821 --> 00:27:22,740
by the War of the Currents.

402
00:27:22,741 --> 00:27:25,880
In a grand gesture to keep the company afloat,

403
00:27:25,881 --> 00:27:28,420
Tesla tore up his royalties contract,

404
00:27:28,421 --> 00:27:31,140
claiming better inventions lay ahead.

405
00:27:31,141 --> 00:27:33,280
Had the contract been fulfilled,

406
00:27:33,281 --> 00:27:36,020
Tesla would have been a millionaire

407
00:27:36,021 --> 00:27:40,720
and been free of financial worries the rest of his life.

408
00:27:40,721 --> 00:27:44,660
Money does not represent such a value as men have placed upon it.

409
00:27:45,020 --> 00:27:47,760
All my money has been invested into experiments

410
00:27:47,761 --> 00:27:50,220
with which I have made new discoveries,

411
00:27:50,560 --> 00:27:53,940
enabling mankind to have a little easier life.

412
00:27:55,800 --> 00:27:57,540
After the triumph over Edison

413
00:27:57,541 --> 00:27:59,420
and the success of his alternating current,

414
00:28:00,360 --> 00:28:01,860
Nikola Tesla was a celebrity.

415
00:28:03,820 --> 00:28:06,120
And he's friends with Mark Twain.

416
00:28:06,300 --> 00:28:08,340
He's friends with all manner of ambassadors

417
00:28:08,341 --> 00:28:10,500
and ballerinas and prima donnas.

418
00:28:10,720 --> 00:28:13,020
And he's really kind of at the height of society.

419
00:28:13,900 --> 00:28:15,980
But genius often comes with a price.

420
00:28:17,340 --> 00:28:19,340
Tesla suffered from bizarre compulsions,

421
00:28:19,520 --> 00:28:22,620
like his consuming need to rescue injured pigeons

422
00:28:22,621 --> 00:28:24,260
and nurse them back to health.

423
00:28:24,940 --> 00:28:28,000
He has an infirmary for them right outside his bedroom window

424
00:28:28,001 --> 00:28:29,660
where he's trying to heal, you know,

425
00:28:29,700 --> 00:28:31,320
some that have broken wings, broken legs.

426
00:28:31,321 --> 00:28:35,700
And in fact, at one point, he actually referred to one bird as his wife.

427
00:28:35,701 --> 00:28:38,040
and he said, you know, when she died,

428
00:28:38,480 --> 00:28:40,800
the inventive spirit left him.

429
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:43,620
Tesla had a fear of germs

430
00:28:43,621 --> 00:28:46,220
that compelled him to wash his hands constantly.

431
00:28:47,200 --> 00:28:48,840
He did things in threes

432
00:28:48,841 --> 00:28:50,800
and was adamant about living in a room

433
00:28:50,801 --> 00:28:51,900
at the New Yorker Hotel

434
00:28:51,901 --> 00:28:53,960
with a number divisible by three.

435
00:28:55,220 --> 00:28:57,740
He demanded the room 3327

436
00:28:57,741 --> 00:28:59,260
where he'd lived for 10 years.

437
00:28:59,720 --> 00:29:02,420
He always requested nine napkins

438
00:29:02,421 --> 00:29:05,000
and if he had a bread or something,

439
00:29:05,001 --> 00:29:08,200
It could be cut into nine, three times three pieces.

440
00:29:08,940 --> 00:29:14,800
Even the number of dishes he had or the number of towels he received in his room was usually three or nine.

441
00:29:15,940 --> 00:29:18,600
Tesla never married and claimed he was celibate.

442
00:29:19,380 --> 00:29:23,400
He had a horrible affliction against women with pierced ears.

443
00:29:23,600 --> 00:29:25,980
He couldn't stand the sight of pierced ears and jewelry.

444
00:29:26,420 --> 00:29:29,420
And pearl earrings, he said, just set his teeth on edge.

445
00:29:29,460 --> 00:29:32,840
It was almost like fingers on a chalkboard for him to see pearl earrings.

446
00:29:32,841 --> 00:29:36,680
Even with his list of obsessions and compulsions,

447
00:29:36,681 --> 00:29:40,680
Tesla had always been able to tap into his remarkable inventive intellect.

448
00:29:42,680 --> 00:29:46,620
In 1898, he created the remote control

449
00:29:46,621 --> 00:29:50,400
when he demonstrated a six-foot-long radio wave-controlled boat

450
00:29:50,401 --> 00:29:51,900
at Madison Square Garden.

451
00:29:59,800 --> 00:30:02,480
On a quiet lake in Riverside, California,

452
00:30:02,840 --> 00:30:05,080
Tesla's work is alive and well,

453
00:30:05,081 --> 00:30:08,520
as a fleet of remote-controlled model boats sets sail.

454
00:30:08,521 --> 00:30:16,080
This is very much like Tesla did a hundred years ago.

455
00:30:16,081 --> 00:30:20,660
Inside these boats are the basic components

456
00:30:20,661 --> 00:30:23,960
of remote control Tesla conceived at the turn of the 20th century.

457
00:30:23,961 --> 00:30:32,240
A battery inside the remote control device sends power

458
00:30:32,241 --> 00:30:36,780
to an oscillator that converts it into a radio wave pulse.

459
00:30:36,781 --> 00:30:40,120
The radio wave is transmitted to a small receiver on the boat,

460
00:30:40,121 --> 00:30:42,520
which sends the electric pulse to a motor.

461
00:30:42,521 --> 00:30:44,860
Varying the pulse of the radio wave from the transmitter

462
00:30:44,861 --> 00:30:47,160
to the receiver causes the motor arms

463
00:30:47,161 --> 00:30:48,620
to move in different directions.

464
00:30:52,860 --> 00:30:56,560
Using a joystick, the operator controls the radio pulse signal

465
00:30:56,561 --> 00:30:57,600
and controls the boat.

466
00:31:02,240 --> 00:31:06,600
Tesla's remote control principles are seen today in everything from televisions

467
00:31:06,601 --> 00:31:13,060
to unmanned military predator drones used for battlefield reconnaissance

468
00:31:13,061 --> 00:31:16,680
to satellites operating in space.

469
00:31:18,860 --> 00:31:24,600
Tesla's shocking experiments continue now on Modern Marvel's Mad Electricity Plus.

470
00:31:25,600 --> 00:31:29,600
One of the most powerful forces in nature is an earthquake.

471
00:31:30,540 --> 00:31:32,860
Suppose that power was harnessed as a weapon.

472
00:31:36,080 --> 00:31:38,860
Some believe Tesla may have discovered a way.

473
00:31:39,520 --> 00:31:42,000
The Tesla earthquake machine was a mechanical oscillator,

474
00:31:42,260 --> 00:31:44,700
which is basically a machine that produces vibrations.

475
00:31:45,520 --> 00:31:47,440
And by producing these vibrations,

476
00:31:47,600 --> 00:31:50,940
the machine could be made to resonate with different structures.

477
00:31:51,880 --> 00:31:54,660
The oscillator machine is based on the principle

478
00:31:54,661 --> 00:31:58,680
that every substance, when stimulated, has a resonant frequency.

479
00:31:58,681 --> 00:32:04,500
If the frequency is matched and amplified by an outside force, such as the oscillator machine,

480
00:32:05,100 --> 00:32:07,760
any material can be literally shaken to pieces.

481
00:32:09,620 --> 00:32:15,780
The destruction of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge in Washington in 1940 demonstrates this principle.

482
00:32:16,800 --> 00:32:20,280
The winds whipping around the bridge hit just the right frequency,

483
00:32:20,860 --> 00:32:25,720
causing the steel bridge to sway violently in a rhythmic pattern that finally broke it apart.

484
00:32:28,680 --> 00:32:34,300
To demonstrate the power of resonance, we're going to see what happens to a wine glass

485
00:32:34,301 --> 00:32:36,560
as it vibrates to its breaking point.

486
00:32:36,561 --> 00:32:43,040
First, I'll start the glass vibrating and use a microphone connected to an oscilloscope

487
00:32:43,041 --> 00:32:46,240
to see what frequency the glass vibrates at.

488
00:32:46,241 --> 00:32:54,320
I'll then set that frequency on a signal generator, which is going to play back through an amplifier

489
00:32:54,321 --> 00:32:56,200
into a speaker.

490
00:32:56,201 --> 00:32:58,700
As the glass is going to be moving more than 400 times

491
00:32:58,701 --> 00:33:02,020
per second, we will be using a strobe light in order to slow

492
00:33:02,021 --> 00:33:03,740
down the motion.

493
00:33:03,741 --> 00:33:07,980
Now, this is going to be loud, so I have to wear some protection.

494
00:33:07,981 --> 00:33:10,340
As the vibrations increase in intensity,

495
00:33:10,341 --> 00:33:12,620
the glass will start to move more and more.

496
00:33:12,621 --> 00:33:15,120
And eventually, it can't take it anymore.

497
00:33:15,121 --> 00:33:17,960
So as I turn up the volume, the glass will break.

498
00:33:41,523 --> 00:33:47,420
So what we saw was like a mini earthquake, and Tesla's earthquake machine would be able

499
00:33:47,421 --> 00:33:49,660
to do this, but on a much larger scale.

500
00:33:49,661 --> 00:33:56,940
In 1898, Tesla claimed to have created an oscillator with an adjustable frequency that

501
00:33:56,941 --> 00:33:58,948
was no bigger than an alarm clock.

502
00:33:58,949 --> 00:34:01,900
And he wrote of an attempt to use it on a building

503
00:34:01,901 --> 00:34:03,140
under construction.

504
00:34:03,141 --> 00:34:07,927
In a few minutes, I felt the building beginning to tremble.

505
00:34:07,928 --> 00:34:10,280
Ten minutes more, and I could

506
00:34:10,281 --> 00:34:11,888
have laid it in the street.

507
00:34:11,889 --> 00:34:15,820
And with the same device, I could have dropped the Brooklyn Bridge

508
00:34:15,821 --> 00:34:18,520
into the East River in less than an hour.

509
00:34:20,860 --> 00:34:22,760
Perhaps this opened a Pandora's box

510
00:34:22,761 --> 00:34:24,820
during later experiments with the device.

511
00:34:27,040 --> 00:34:31,900
There's a legend that he started up his little oscillator

512
00:34:31,901 --> 00:34:34,200
or what some call the earthquake machine

513
00:34:34,201 --> 00:34:36,680
and he started vibrating lower Manhattan.

514
00:34:36,681 --> 00:34:39,620
And they called went out to the police department

515
00:34:39,621 --> 00:34:42,580
and the police came running, they were pounding on the door.

516
00:34:42,581 --> 00:34:44,880
They broke into his lab and they saw him

517
00:34:44,881 --> 00:34:46,380
with a sledgehammer smashing the machine,

518
00:34:46,381 --> 00:34:48,260
the switch got stuck, he couldn't turn it off.

519
00:34:48,261 --> 00:34:50,660
And he almost brought down some buildings in the neighborhood.

520
00:34:52,220 --> 00:34:56,120
In 1934, Tesla conceived a death ray.

521
00:34:57,520 --> 00:35:00,960
His idea was to blast concentrated beams of particles

522
00:35:00,961 --> 00:35:03,260
charged with millions of volts of electricity

523
00:35:03,261 --> 00:35:07,460
through the air, which could down fleets of enemy aircraft

524
00:35:07,461 --> 00:35:10,400
at a distance of 250 miles.

525
00:35:10,401 --> 00:35:16,940
Tesla terrified people with this idea of the death ray.

526
00:35:16,941 --> 00:35:21,200
I mean, even the name, Death Ray, it sounds like it's science fiction, right?

527
00:35:21,201 --> 00:35:27,160
Tesla spent his later years in two small rooms in the New Yorker Hotel.

528
00:35:27,161 --> 00:35:33,360
Today, room 3327, one of his rooms, showed the cramped space he occupied for the last

529
00:35:33,361 --> 00:35:36,320
ten years of his life.

530
00:35:36,321 --> 00:35:40,360
Tesla died alone in his room at the hotel.

531
00:35:40,361 --> 00:35:44,360
They weren't even sure exactly when he died because people hadn't seen him for a couple

532
00:35:44,361 --> 00:35:45,360
of days.

533
00:35:45,361 --> 00:35:52,540
A chambermaid finally entered his room the day after he died and found his body.

534
00:35:52,541 --> 00:35:58,600
But when his death at the age of 86 was discovered, the United States government took control

535
00:35:58,601 --> 00:36:02,260
of his scientific papers.

536
00:36:02,261 --> 00:36:07,520
There was a great deal of concern at the time of his death as to what was in his papers

537
00:36:07,521 --> 00:36:08,700
because we were in the middle of World War II.

538
00:36:08,701 --> 00:36:09,800
And there's a great deal of concern.

539
00:36:09,900 --> 00:36:11,160
What if the Nazis get a hold of this?

540
00:36:12,740 --> 00:36:15,880
The government claims that after inspecting Tesla's papers,

541
00:36:16,300 --> 00:36:18,300
they were released in 1952

542
00:36:18,301 --> 00:36:21,220
and later sent to Belgrade, Serbia,

543
00:36:21,480 --> 00:36:22,760
where they now remain,

544
00:36:23,480 --> 00:36:25,520
at the Nikola Tesla Museum.

545
00:36:27,040 --> 00:36:30,900
However, many believe some of his papers are still missing.

546
00:36:33,020 --> 00:36:34,720
Who knows what were in these papers?

547
00:36:35,140 --> 00:36:36,580
You know, maybe they are very sensitive.

548
00:36:36,780 --> 00:36:38,520
Maybe they are the plans for the death ray.

549
00:36:38,521 --> 00:36:41,460
and, you know, I guess that shouldn't be public knowledge.

550
00:36:41,660 --> 00:36:45,060
It's a terrific mystery who took Nikola Tesla's papers.

551
00:36:45,780 --> 00:36:48,120
We may never know what all these papers contained.

552
00:36:51,960 --> 00:36:56,580
The United States consumes nearly 20 million barrels of oil a day,

553
00:36:57,080 --> 00:36:59,480
and gasoline prices fluctuate wildly.

554
00:37:00,880 --> 00:37:04,800
Even a century ago, Tesla saw the future of fossil fuels.

555
00:37:04,801 --> 00:37:13,140
If we use fuel to get our power, we are living on our capital and exhausting it rapidly.

556
00:37:13,141 --> 00:37:17,960
This method is barbarous and wantonly wasteful, and will have to be stopped in the interest

557
00:37:17,961 --> 00:37:21,560
of coming generations.

558
00:37:21,561 --> 00:37:28,860
Tesla was disturbed by the rate at which society was burning up non-renewable fuels.

559
00:37:28,861 --> 00:37:34,620
coal, oil was just coming in then, and he foresaw a time that you would need to go to

560
00:37:34,621 --> 00:37:38,740
some other source of power.

561
00:37:38,741 --> 00:37:43,820
Tesla anticipated the development of what we now call renewable energy.

562
00:37:43,821 --> 00:37:50,960
In 1901, he patented an apparatus for the utilization of radiant energy.

563
00:37:50,961 --> 00:37:58,200
The patent refers to the sun, as well as other sources of radiant energy, like cosmic rays.

564
00:37:58,201 --> 00:38:06,200
Tesla was one of the first people to realize that we have an almost endless supply of energy coming in from space, from our own sun.

565
00:38:06,201 --> 00:38:11,180
I bet he wouldn't be surprised to find out that today all kinds of applications are running on solar power.

566
00:38:12,600 --> 00:38:15,780
Tesla also championed geothermal power.

567
00:38:16,480 --> 00:38:22,400
In 1931, he wrote an article for the New York Times entitled Our Future Mode of Power,

568
00:38:22,900 --> 00:38:25,560
which explored how to utilize the Earth's natural energy.

569
00:38:25,561 --> 00:38:37,780
All that is necessary is to find an economic and speedy way of sinking deep shafts to tap into this enormous geothermal energy.

570
00:38:41,680 --> 00:38:47,460
One cutting-edge carmaker is paying homage to Tesla's green foresight by adopting his name.

571
00:38:48,160 --> 00:38:51,940
Today, Tesla Motors produces the best-selling electric vehicles on the market.

572
00:38:51,941 --> 00:38:57,300
The company's all-electric fleet includes cars assisted by cutting-edge autopilot technology,

573
00:38:57,540 --> 00:39:00,680
a step towards a potentially self-driving vehicle.

574
00:39:01,560 --> 00:39:07,740
The company began cranking out electric vehicles in 2008 with a battery-driven Tesla Roadster,

575
00:39:08,140 --> 00:39:12,260
a vehicle that used a version of its namesake inventor's AC induction motor.

576
00:39:13,120 --> 00:39:20,240
The Roadster could go from 0 to 60 in 3.9 seconds and travel more than 200 miles without a drop of gas.

577
00:39:21,940 --> 00:39:28,940
Tesla was also a green lighting pioneer.

578
00:39:28,941 --> 00:39:35,140
At the 1893 Chicago World's Fair, Tesla displayed a fluorescent bulb that burned much cooler

579
00:39:35,141 --> 00:39:42,040
and lasted longer than Edison's incandescent bulbs, a forerunner of today's longer-lasting,

580
00:39:42,041 --> 00:39:43,460
efficient lighting.

581
00:39:43,461 --> 00:39:47,360
This is an actual Edison light bulb.

582
00:39:47,361 --> 00:39:53,400
This filament's made of carbon, and by passing electricity through it, it becomes heated,

583
00:39:53,401 --> 00:39:57,680
and this heat produces a white light.

584
00:39:57,681 --> 00:40:02,220
More than half of the energy that went into this light bulb produced heat instead of light,

585
00:40:02,221 --> 00:40:04,440
and that made it very inefficient.

586
00:40:04,441 --> 00:40:10,180
Tesla, as a result, developed a lamp on a different principle that didn't produce heat.

587
00:40:10,181 --> 00:40:13,260
It produced light by a gas inside of a bulb.

588
00:40:13,261 --> 00:40:17,700
The electricity enters the bulb through the ends and there's electrodes inside.

589
00:40:17,701 --> 00:40:23,100
And as it enters the bulb, the gas would get excited and ignite inside, lighting the whole

590
00:40:23,101 --> 00:40:29,300
space within the walls of the tube.

591
00:40:29,301 --> 00:40:35,240
The result was the light was brighter, it ran cooler, and it was more efficient than

592
00:40:35,241 --> 00:40:36,540
Edison's bulb.

593
00:40:36,541 --> 00:40:40,280
The modern compact fluorescent light operates on the same principle.

594
00:40:40,281 --> 00:40:43,280
There's gas inside that's excited by a high voltage.

595
00:40:46,280 --> 00:40:49,280
And this is Tesla's principle still used today.

596
00:40:50,280 --> 00:40:54,280
Which other of Tesla's ideas were too far ahead of his time?

597
00:40:55,280 --> 00:41:01,280
History may have neglected this genius, but today he is being rediscovered.

598
00:41:01,281 --> 00:41:05,280
I believe that someday we will reinvestigate Tesla's work

599
00:41:05,281 --> 00:41:08,280
and we will discover the secrets that he was so confident in.

600
00:41:10,280 --> 00:41:17,840
Nikola Tesla made the modern world what it is today and although it took many years for

601
00:41:17,841 --> 00:41:23,220
a lot of people to finally realize it, I think perhaps that is changing now.

602
00:41:23,221 --> 00:41:27,260
The legacy of Tesla is really this creative spirit of the inventor.

603
00:41:27,261 --> 00:41:34,200
To have no bounds on thought, he really believed anything was possible.

604
00:41:34,201 --> 00:41:41,340
Let the future tell the truth and evaluate each one according to their work and accomplishments.

605
00:41:41,341 --> 00:41:43,100
The present is theirs.

606
00:41:43,101 --> 00:41:47,420
The future, for which I really worked, is mine.

607
00:41:47,421 --> 00:41:48,420
Nikola Tesla

